<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Bencoolen</h1><p class="st-meta">1685 CE – 1824 CE · capital Fort Marlborough</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British East India Company pepper settlement on the south-west coast of Sumatra, later governed by Stamford Raffles.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> in 1682, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">English East India Company</a> sought a new source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> and in 1685 founded a post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengkulu">Bencoolen</a> (Bengkulu) by agreement with local chiefs. It became the Company base in the Malay archipelago, and between 1714 and 1719 the garrison built Fort Marlborough, one of the largest British forts in Asia.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The settlement was run by a Company resident and council, later a lieutenant-governor, who forced local farmers to grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> for fixed prices. It was unhealthy and lost money for most of its history, and convicts and slaves from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> were used as labour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Thomas Stamford Raffles</a> governed from 1818 to 1824, abolishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and promoting new crops.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> exchanged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengkulu">Bencoolen</a> for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-malacca">Dutch Malacca</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> took over in 1824 and 1825, folding it into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. Fort Marlborough still stands in Bengkulu.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bencoolen.jpg" alt="Bencoolen" width="320" height="147"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bengkulu_in_Indonesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1685 CE – 1824 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Fort Marlborough</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Thomas Stamford Raffles</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (local), Anglican Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Bencoolen, Fort Marlborough, Bengkulu (British)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1754&amp;focus=bencoolen" aria-label="Bencoolen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7518 2321 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bencoolen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7838 2577L4997 651M7838 2577L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7838 2577h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7838 2577h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1754&amp;focus=bencoolen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
